<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daedalus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daedalus is a craftsman and inventor of Greek mythology, said to have built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a> to hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>. Imprisoned by Minos, he escaped on wings of feathers and wax that he made for himself and his son Icarus, who flew too near the sun and fell into the sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> credited him with the invention of many crafts and with the earliest statues.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daedalus.jpg" alt="Daedalus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaziantep_Zeugma_Museum_Daedalus_mosaic_1873.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Daidalos seems to be attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, the writing system of Mycenaean Greek, in the form da-da-re-jo-de, possibly referring to a sanctuary. A craftsman named Daedalus is first mentioned in roughly 1400 BCE on the Knossian Linear B tablets, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> later names him as the maker of a dancing floor for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>, referring to mythology his audience already knew. His parentage was a later addition, with various authors giving different fathers and mothers. The Athenians rewrote the Cretan-born Daedalus as an Athenian, a grandson of the ancient king Erechtheus who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> only after killing his nephew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Daedalus is widely praised as an inventor, artist and architect, though classical sources disagree on which inventions are his. Pliny's Natural History credits him with carpentry, including the axe, the saw and glue, and he is said to have first made masts and sails for the navy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>. Pausanias attributed to him numerous archaic wooden cult figures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, in the Meno, uses his moving statues as a metaphor for true understanding, which must be shackled in place if it is not to wander off. Daedalic sculpture takes its name from him.</p><p>In the myths he built a hollow wooden cow so that Pasiphaë, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>, could mate with a bull sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>; she bore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>, which Minos imprisoned in the Labyrinth Daedalus built. When Minos imprisoned him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> says, he made wings of feathers, thread and beeswax. His son Icarus disobeyed his warning, flew too close to the Sun and fell into the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>According to the tradition, Daedalus was jealous of his nephew, called Perdix, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talos">Talos</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mound-key">Calos</a>, who invented the saw and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compasses</a>, and tried to murder him by throwing him from the Acropolis. Athena saved the boy and turned him into a partridge, and Daedalus fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. He later reached Camicus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the protection of King Cocalus.</p><p>Accounts of his death differ: a snake bite at Telmessos, a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> where he was worshipped, or a water snake in Lycia. These anecdotes are literary and late. Modern interpreters, among them Edith Hamilton, read him as an example of practical intelligence guided by restraint, set against the recklessness of Icarus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daedalus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The myths of Minos, the lawgiver king of Knossos, of <b>Daedalus</b> and the Labyrinth, of Theseus, Ariadne and the Minotaur, circulated throughout the Greek world, and the city deliberately advertised them on its coinage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greeks remembered Bronze Age Crete in myth: Minos the lawgiver king, the Labyrinth built by <b>Daedalus</b>, and the Minotaur killed by Theseus with the help of Ariadne.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Early Greek writers wondered at the towers, calling them daidaleia after <b>Daedalus</b>, who was said to have come to Sardinia after building the labyrinth in Crete.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
